Airline Reservation System

Problem Statement

An airline wants a database to manage flight schedules, passenger bookings, and seat allocation across multiple airports and aircraft, including support for connecting flights.

System Requirements

1. Passenger Passenger ID, name, DOB, passport number, contact info.

2. Flight - Flight number, source airport, destination airport, departure time, arrival time, aircraft assigned.

3. Airport - Airport code, name, city, country.

4. Aircraft - Aircraft ID, model, total seating capacity.

5. Booking - Links Passenger and Flight; booking reference number, seat number, class (Economy/Business), booking date, fare.

6. Payment - Linked to Booking; amount, mode, status.

Design Constraints

Airport participates in the Flight relationship twice - as source and as destination. This is a recursive relationship on Airport (via Flight) and must be modeled with two distinct foreign keys (source_airport_id, destination_airport_id) referencing the same Airport

A passenger can book multiple flights; a flight carries multiple passengers - Booking is the associative entity.

Model multi-leg/connecting journeys: a single reservation (trip) may consist of multiple Flight+Booking combinations linked by a common trip/itinerary ID.

Seat number must be unique per flight (composite uniqueness constraint on flight id +

seat_number).

Aircraft is assigned to a flight, and the same aircraft can be reused for different flights at different times (not simultaneously).

Deliverables

1. E-R diagram showing the recursive Airport relationship (source/destination) and the Booking associative entity.

2. Relational schema (3NF) with PK/FK, including composite uniqueness constraints.

3. SQL DDL/DML with sample data (5+ airports, 10+ flights, 15+ passengers, bookings including at least one connecting itinerary).

4. Queries: all flights from a given source to destination; passengers on a specific flight; available seats on a flight; total revenue per flight; passengers with connecting itineraries.

5. Documentation